====Usage notes====
{{sense|"to say", for direct quotes}}
* Using {{m|ko||하고}} is more colloquial and has a nuance of more intimate or faithful quoting than using {{m|ko|-이라고}}. For example, speakers use {{m|ko||하고}} when imitating the actual way something was said (e.g. someone's accent, prosody, etc.), and {{m|ko|-이라고}} when quoting written material.<ref>{{cite-book|title=ko:한국어 인용구문의 연구|trans-title=A study of Korean quotative constructions|year=1995|author=ko:안경화|publisher=Seoul National University (PhD)}}</ref>
*: {{uxi|ko|[[나]][[-보고]] "[[야]] [[너]] [[어디]][[-이다|-야]]?" '''하고''' [[묻다|묻는]] [[거]][[-야]].|t=He was like, "Hey, where are you?"}}
*: {{uxi|ko|[[촉수엄금]][[-이라고]] [[적다|적혀]][[있다|-있었다]].|t=It was written, "Do not touch."}}
{{q|compounds}}
* As Korean verbs and adjectives are a fully {{w|closed class}}, all newly borrowed or coined verbs and adjectives must be formed by {{m|ko|하다}}.
{{q|contractions}}
* In compound verbs or adjectives, the stem can be contracted. This often has a literary nuance.
** After [[obstruent]]-final stems, the entire stem {{m|ko||하-}} is dropped.
**: {{uxi|ko|[[생각하다|생각-하]][[-지]] [[않다|않았다]] > [[생각]][[-지]] [[않다|않았다]]|t=one did not think}}
**: {{uxi|ko|[[깨끗하다|깨끗-하]][[-고]] > [[깨끗]][[-고]]|t=it is clean, and...}}
** After [[sonorant]]-final stems, only the vowel is dropped and the initial {{m|ko|ㅎ}} merges with the following suffix, so that suffixes beginning with the consonants {{m|ko|ㄱ}}, {{m|ko|ㄷ}}, and {{m|ko|ㅈ}} are [[aspirate]]d to begin with {{m|ko|ㅋ}}, {{m|ko|ㅌ}}, and {{m|ko|ㅊ}}:
**: {{uxi|ko|[[고요하다|고요-하]][[-지]] [[않다|않았다]] > [[고요]][[-치]] [[않다|않았다]]|t=(it) was not calm}}
**: {{uxi|ko|[[결단하다|결단-하]][[-고]] > [[결단]][[-코]]|t=determinedly; literally "while being determined..."}}
**: {{uxi|ko|[[불쌍하다|불쌍-하]][[-다]] > [[불쌍]]-타|t=(it) is pitiful}}
* For some of the adverbs formed by such a contraction of conjugated forms, the presumed original verb or adjective is no longer widely used, if at all. For example, the adverb {{m|ko|기어(期於)코|t=no matter what; in the end}} has no corresponding adjective {{m|ko||*기어(期於)하다}}.
* Some of these contracted forms have undergone semantic shifts and are no longer fully equivalent to the uncontracted forms. For example, {{m|ko|무심(無心)코|t=thoughtlessly}} cannot be replaced by {{m|ko||무심(無心)-하고|t=[one] is careless, and...}}.
* Contraction is more common for connective suffixes and less common for sentence-final ones, especially informal ones.
* Contraction is more common in {{w|Gyeongsang dialect}} than in Standard Seoul Korean.
{{q|contraction in indirect quotes}}
* In indirect quotes, the entire stem {{m|ko||하-}} is commonly dropped.
*: {{uxi|ko|[[쉬다|쉬]][[-자]]([[-고#quotative|-고]]) '''하'''[[-는]] [[소리]] > [[쉬다|쉬]][[-자]][[-는]] [[소리]]|t=her '''say'''ing we should rest}}
*: {{uxi|ko|[[없다|없]][[-다]]([[-고#quotative|-고]]) '''하'''[[-더라]] > [[없]][[-다]][[-더라]]|t=He '''said''' it wasn't there.}}
{{q|conjugation}}
* In less formal language, the infinitive {{m|ko||하여}} is always contracted to {{m|ko|해#하여|해}}.
* Because the [[w:Korean speech levels#Hae-che|intimate style]] is never formal by definition, {{m|ko||하여}} can never replace {{m|ko|해}} in such contexts.

