===Etymology 9===
South Korean reading of various Chinese characters in isolation or as the first element of a compound, and also the reading in most dialects in 1945, excluding {{w|Pyongan dialect|Pyongan}} and {{w|Yukjin dialect|Yukjin}}, where they are pronounced in this position as {{m|ko|네}} in Pyongan or as {{m|ko|녜}} in Yukjin.

From {{inh|ko|okm|-}} {{okm-inline|례|lyey}}. When preceded by another character in a compound, they retain the original {{m|ko|례}} form.

In the North Korean standard, they are always read as {{m|ko|례}}, but this is an artificial imposition intended to standardize Sino-Korean readings, which did not reflect any major dialect's pronunciation in 1945.

