Modern South Korean reading of various Chinese characters in isolation or as the first element of a compound, which was also true of most dialects (both North and South) in 1945. From {{inh|ko|okm|-}} {{okm-inline|랄|lal}}; when preceded by another character in a compound, they retain the original {{m|ko|랄}} form.

In the North Korean standard, they are always read as {{m|ko|랄}}, but this is an artificial imposition intended to standardize Sino-Korean readings, which did not reflect any major dialect's pronunciation in 1945.

