{{anchor|rain}}
{{ko-etym-native|gy|霏微|*piWi}} Hence the word was originally disyllabic; Alexander Vovin believes it was borrowed from Tungusic,<ref>{{cite-journal|title=Pre-Hankul Materials, Koreo-Japonic, and Altaic|author={{w|Alexander Vovin}}|journal=Korean Studies|year=2000|volume=24|pages=142—155}}</ref> in this case, it would ultimately be from {{der|ko|tuw-pro|*pigi-|t=to blow; rain}}.

However, Hyunkyu Shin suggests that remaining editions of ''Jīlín lèishì'' misinterpreted the original, that 微 was originally a part of the following explanation "微雪下曰
嫩耻" (small amount of snowfall is called "''*nwun ti''"), and that "rain" should correspond to only 霏 (Yale: ''*pi'').<ref>{{cite-journal|title=鷄林類事 高麗方言 연구에 제시된 구조 교정 一考察|year=2023|journal=영주어문|volume=53|pages=89-125}}</ref>

