# {{lb|ko|euphemistic|_|but not honorific}} to [[pass away]]
#* {{quote-song|ko|year=2025|title=ko:기억하리|trans-title=We Will Remember|text=^[[기억하다|기억하]][[-리]] [[존엄]][[-과]] [[명예]] [[지키다|지켜]][[내다|낸]] [[영웅]][[-들]]<br>^[[기억하다|기억하]][[-리]] [[조국]][[-을]] [[위해]] '''간''' [[그]] [[생]][[-들]][[-을]]|t=}}

====Usage notes====
* '''Transitivity''': Korean verbs of movement are variably intransitive and transitive:
** If the destination of the movement is not a space or only a small one, it is intransitive: {{m|ko|[[나무]][[-에]] '''가다'''|t=to go to a tree}}, {{m|ko|[[내]] [[방]][[-에]] '''가다'''|t=to go to my room}}.
** If the destination of the movement is an extended space into which one enters, it can be both transitive and intransitive with no significant change in meaning: {{m|ko|^[[중국]][[-으로]] '''가다'''|t=to go to China}}, {{m|ko|^[[중국]][[-을]] '''가다'''|t=to go to China}}. But intransitive use is rather more common, and transitive use may be understood as [[emphatic]].
** If the object is a path that one goes on, it is transitive: {{m|ko|[[길]][[-을]] '''가다'''|t=to go on a road}}, {{m|ko|[[인도]][[-를]] '''가다'''|t=to go on the pavement}}.
** With the exception of "to last", which is always transitive, the various extended senses are intransitive.
* '''Conjugation''': This verb has two forms for the plain-style {{m|ko|해라체}} imperative. The blunt form is regular: {{m|ko|가라|t=Go!}}. The less blunt form is irregular: {{m|ko|가거라|t=Go (well)!}}. Other verbs with the irregular non-blunt form include {{m|ko|일어나다|t=to get up}}, {{m|ko|자라다|t=to grow}}, {{m|ko|자다|t=to sleep}}, and compound verbs ending in this verb. Historically, this reflects the perseverance of the Middle Korean suffix {{okm-inline|-거-|-ke-}}.

