==Middle Korean==

===Pronunciation===
{{okm-IPA|tone=H!H}}

===Verb===
{{head|okm|verb|head=가다〮|tr=kàtá|infinitive|가〮|sequential|가니〮}}

# {{lb|okm|intransitive}} to [[go]] (especially somewhere for a certain [[purpose]])
#: {{ant|okm|오다〮|t=to come|tr=wòtá}}
#* {{quote-book|okm|title=ko:번역노걸대상|year=1517|page=10a|passage=^[[그저]] [[뎌|->>>뎨]] '''가''' [[자다|자-고]] '''가-져'''.|t=Let's just '''go''' there and sleep before '''going''' (to the destination).|origlang=zh|origtext=zh:只那裏宿去。}}
# {{lb|okm|auxiliary|with {{m|okm|어〮|tr=-é}}}} {{n-g|Marks a [[continuous]] action that either stretches from the present into the future, or involves physical movement away from the speaker.}}
#: {{ant|okm|오다〮|pos=continuous action from the past or towards the speaker|tr=wòtá}}
#* {{quote-book|okm|title=ko:남명집언해|year=1482|page=5a|passage=^殘花(잔화)-ᄂᆞᆫ [[ᄒᆞ마]] '''[[없다|업->서]] 가-ᄂᆞᆫ''' [[곶|고->지-라]].|t='殘花' is a flower that will soon cease to exist.}}

====Usage notes====
Middle Korean had two (possibly three) verbs equivalent to Modern {{m|ko|가다|t=to go}} or English "to go": {{m|okm|가다〮|tr=kàtá}}, and {{m|okm|녀다〮|tr=nyètá}} / {{m|okm|니다〮|tr=nìtá}}. The latter appears only in compound verbs and before the suffix {{m|okm|-거-|tr=-ke-}}. Some scholars believe that {{m|okm|녀다〮|tr=nyètá}} and {{m|okm|니다〮|tr=nìtá}} were separate verbs.

It is believed that {{m|okm||가다〮|tr=kàtá}} had a stronger emphasis on the purpose of the movement, while {{m|okm|녀다〮|tr=nyètá}} / {{m|okm|니다〮|tr=nìtá}} had a stronger emphasis on the physical movement itself. For example, only {{m|okm||가다〮|tr=kàtá}} is attested with a human indirect object (i.e. expressing "to go to a person [for something]"); the indirect objects taken by {{m|okm|녀다〮|tr=nyètá}} / {{m|okm|니다〮|tr=nìtá}} are all real or metaphorical spaces. However, both could be used to translate the same Chinese verb, showing that the semantic distinction was not so strict.

