===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|gy|烏|wo-|nodot=y}}, perhaps from earlier {{IPAfont|<*wòn->}}.

{{ko-etym-native|yb|오다〮|wò-tá|hangul=y}}

{{box|
There is strong evidence that at some ancient stage, the root of the verb was {{IPAfont|<*wòn->}} with a final nasal (cf. {{ko-ref|Vovin 2007, pp. 27—28}}). Consider:
* The irregular Middle Korean imperative {{okm-l|오〮나라〮|wónàlá|3=come!}} is traditionally analyzed as involving a suffix {{okm-l|-나〮-|-ná-}} which appears solely with this verb. But this conjugation would be regular if {{IPAfont|<*wòn->}} was the root, with the conjugation simply involving the suffix {{okm-l|-아〮-|-á-}} attested for other verbs.
* The possibly dialectal Middle Korean form {{okm-l|오〮나-도〮|wónàtwó|3=even if he comes}} is attested once, instead of expected {{okm-l|오〮아도〮|wóàtwó|3=even if he comes}}. This is again regular if {{IPAfont|<*wòn->}} was the original root.
* In western {{w|Chungcheong dialect}}, the infinitive form of {{m-self|ko|오다}} is {{m|ko||오느}}, {{m|ko||오노}}, {{m|ko||오누}}, or {{m|ko||오나}}, instead of expected {{m|ko||와}}.<ref>{{cite-book|ko|title=ko:국어 방언 문법|trans-title=Korean Dialectal Grammar|pages=368—370|author=ko:이기갑|year=2003|publisher=Taehaksa|location=Seoul, Republic of Korea|isbn=89-7626-818-0}}</ref>
* In {{cog|jje|-}}, the traditional infinitive form of {{m|jje|오다}} is {{m|jje||오란}} instead of expected {{m|jje||*완}}, which may involve conflation of final {{IPAfont|*-n}} with {{IPAfont|*-l}}.|Etymological details}}

===Pronunciation===
{{ko-IPA|a=y}}

===Verb===
{{ko-verb}}

# to [[come]] {{gl|to move towards the [[speaker]]}}
#: {{ant|ko|가다|t=to [[go]]}}
#: {{uxi|ko|[[파티]][[-에]] '''와'''[[주다|-줘서]] [[고맙다|고마워]].|Thanks for '''coming''' to the party.}}
#: {{uxi|ko|^[[어떻게]] '''오셨어'''[[-요]]?|What brings you here?|q=stockphrase, used in the sense "how can I help you?"}}
#: {{uxi|ko|[[여기]][[-로]] '''오는''' [[사람]][[-들]]|t=the people '''coming''' here}}
#: {{uxi|ko|'''오기'''[[-를]] [[잘하다|잘했다]]|made the right choice in '''coming'''; glad that one '''came'''}}
# {{lb|ko|of rain, snow, etc.}} to [[fall]]
#: {{syn|ko|내리다}}
#: {{uxi|ko|[[비]][[-가]] '''온다'''.|It's raining.|lit=Rain '''comes'''.}}
# {{lb|ko|auxiliary|with {{m|ko|-어|tr=-}}}} {{n-g|Marks a [[continuous]] action that stretches from the past into the present.}}
#: {{ant|ko|가다}}
#: {{uxi|ko|[[오랫동안]] ^[[인도]][[-에]] [[살다|살아]]'''-왔다'''.|t=He '''has''' lived in India for a long time.}}

====Usage notes====
* Korean equivalents of English expressions that use the verb “to come” in the present tense from the perspective of the listener, not the speaker, usually use the verb {{l|ko|가다|t=to go}}; that is, “to come” is inherently perceived as moving towards the speaker’s current position. For example, “I’m coming right now” in Korean is {{m|ko|[[나]] [[지금]] [[가다|가고]] [[있다|있어]]|lit=I’m going right now}}.
* {{m-self|ko|오다}} has two forms for the {{l|ko|해라체}} imperative. The blunt form is regular: {{l|ko|와라|t=come!}}. The less blunt form is irregular: {{l|ko|오너라|t=(please) come!}}. Other verbs with the irregular non-blunt form are {{l|ko|일어나다|t=to get up}}, {{l|ko|자라다|t=to grow}}, {{l|ko|자다|t=to sleep}}, {{l|ko|가다|t=to go}}, and compound verbs ending in {{l|ko|-가다}}.

