===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ko|가찹다|가직다|가적다|가죽다|개직다|개작다}} {{q|all dialectal}}

===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|ss|갓갑〯다〮|kàskǎp-tá|dot=,}} most plausibly from the unattested verbal root {{okm-l|*가ᄌᆞᆨ-|*kacok-|5=probably "to approach"|link=no}} + {{okm-l|아ᇦ〯|-ǎW|5=adjective-deriving suffix}}. Dialects commonly preserve the original root.<ref>{{cite-journal|year=1983|title=ko:재구와 방언분화|trans-title=Reconstructions and dialectal divergence|author={{lang|ko|이승재}} (Yi Seung-jae)|journal=Gugeohak|volume=12}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{ko-IPA}}

===Adjective===
{{ko-adj|irreg=y}}

# to be [[close]], to be [[near]] {{q|in distance, time, emotional rapport, kinship, etc.}}
#: {{ant|ko|멀다}}
#: {{uxi|ko|'''가까운''' [[친척]]|t='''close''' relatives}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[새로]] [[이사하다|이사한]] [[집]][[-은]] [[학교]][[-와]] '''가깝다'''.|t=The school '''is close''' to our new house.}}

====Usage notes====
In the South Korean standard language, ㅂ-irregular [[polysyllabic]] words like 가깝다 differ from [[monosyllabic]] ones like [[돕다]] in that their conjugation ignores [[vowel harmony]]: 가까워 vs. 도와. But in the North Korean standard language (and in the South before 1988), vowel harmony applies regardless of the number of syllables: 가까와. This is one of the [[w:North–South differences in the Korean language#ㅂ_p-irregular_inflections|North–South differences in the Korean language]]

