===Etymology 1===
{{ko-etym-native|ss|봄〮|pwóm}} The etymology beyond Middle Korean is not clear:

* Middle Korean nouns ending in {{IPAfont|/-m/}} are often substantives of verbs, but the only relevant verb is {{okm-l|보다〮|pwò-tá|3=to see}}, which seems semantically unrelated.
* Some online sources claim a connection to the element {{okm-l|봄|pwom}} in the Middle Korean compound verb {{okm-l|봄놀다|pwom-nwolta|to romp; to spring about in play}}, whose second element is {{okm-l|놀〯다〮|nwǒltá|3=to play}}. If valid, the semantics would be similar to {{ncog|en|spring}}. It is not clear whether this has been suggested in academia.
:However, it appears more likely that the element {{okm-l|봄|pwom}} is an [[allomorph]] of {{m|okm|봅-}}, nasalized by the following {{IPAfont|/n/}}, as indicated by the variant spelling {{m|okm|봅놀다}}; compare ablaut variant {{okm-l|붑괴다|pwup-kwoyta|to seethe}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|ko|title=ko:'봄놀다'와 '붑괴다'에 대한 어원론적 형태 분석|author=ko:박진호(朴鎭浩)|date=September 30, 2009|journal=Hyeongtaeron|volume=2|issue=2|pages=209—214}}</ref>
* It has been tentatively proposed that this is an ancient pre-Sino-Korean borrowing from {{der|ko|och|-}} {{och-l|芃|tr=*[b]ˤ[o]m|luxuriant growth (of foliage)}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|title=ko:상고 중국어 음운현상에서 본 한국어 어원|author=ko:김태경|date=December 2014|pages=71—90|journal=Junggugeo Munhak Nonjip|volume=89}}</ref>

