# [[not]]
#: {{ux|ko|'''안''' [[먹다|먹어]]?|Won’t [you] eat?}}
#: {{ux|ko|'''안''' [[먹다|먹어]]!|[I] won’t eat!}}

=====Usage notes=====
The related auxiliary verb {{m|ko|않다}} is used to negate a clause, and has a more formal connotation. Compare:

* {{ux|ko|[[야]], [[나]] '''안''' [[먹다|먹었어]].|Hey, I didn't eat.}}
* {{ux|ko|[[교수]][[-님]], [[저]][[-는]] [[먹다|먹지]] [[않다|않았습니다]].|I didn't eat, Professor.}}

Certain terms or expressions have a strong (sometimes obligatory) preference for one negation type or another. For example, adjectives derived from {{m|ko|-스럽다}} are usually negated by {{m|ko|않다}}, as do inherently negative verbs such as {{m|ko|없다|t=to not have}} and {{m|ko|모르다|t=to not know}}. By contrast, negated rhetorical questions obligatorily require {{m|ko|안}}.

{{m|ko|안|t=not}} negates only the verb or adjective, whereas {{m|ko|않다}} negates the entire embedded clause. While this difference is often not semantically meaningful, it can also lead to contrasting meanings, such as when the particle {{m|ko|만|t=only}} is involved:

* {{ux|ko|[[사과]][[-만]] '''안''' [[먹다|먹어]].|Apples are the only thing they don't eat.}}
* {{ux|ko|[[사과]][[-만]] [[먹다|먹지]] '''않아'''.|They don't only eat apples [but they eat other things too].}}

In the first case, only the verb {{m|ko|먹다|t=to eat}} is negated. In the latter, the entire clause {{m|ko|[[사과]][[-만]] [[먹다]]|t=to eat only apples}} is negated.

