===Etymology 1===
{{anchor|bird}}
{{ko-attest|oko-lat|賽|계림유사}}. {{ko-etym-native|yb|새〯|sǎy|hangul=y|dot=,}} the rising pitch implying an earlier bisyllabic form {{lang|ko|*saCi}}, which is reflected in {{cog|jje|생이}} and {{m|jje|상이}}, and dialectal {{m|ko|사이|pos=Gyeonggi, South Chungcheong, Hwanghae}}.

{{ko-etym-native|ggb|새〯|sǎy|also=y|dot=,}} where it was glossed as "sparrow".

Compare also {{cog|och|-}} {{och-l|雀|sparrow}}.

In the Jilin Leishi as well as the Gugeupganibang eonhae, the Korean name for sparrow was labeled as {{m|ko|새}}. This implies that the old meaning of {{m|ko|새}} is sparrow, but {{m|ko|새}} was borrowed for other terms referring to birds. Also note the compound {{m|ko|새매|t=sparrowhawk}}, which at face value would redundantly mean "bird-hawk", but if {{m|ko|새}} meant sparrow, would indeed be "sparrow-hawk" (harkening to the sparrowhawk's prey). Finally, {{m|ko|참새}}, the modern word for sparrow, literally translates as "true-''sae''".

