두꺼비:
==Korean==
[[Image:Bufo gargarizans frontal.jpg|thumb|'''두꺼비''' (dukkeobi)]]

===Etymology===
Usually believed to be equivalent to {{suffix|ko|[[두껍다|두껍—]]|t1=to be thick|이|pos2=noun suffix|id2=noun suffix}}, referring to the toad's thick skin.<ref>{{cite-book|title=ko:우리말 어원사전|editor=ko:김민수|year=1997|isbn=9788976262776|publisher=Taehaksa|page=276}}</ref>

{{ko-etym-native|hyg|豆何非|*twuhapi}} {{ko-etym-native|hh|hangul=y|두텁|twùthèp|dot=,}} corresponding to Middle Korean {{m|okm|두텁〯다〮|tr=twuthěptá|t=to be thick}}. {{ko-etym-native|''Saseong tonghae'' ({{lang|ko|四聲通解 / 사성통해}})|둗거비〮|twùtkèpí|1517|also=y|dot=,}} which is the first attestation of a form directly ancestral to the modern.

The ancestral form for the adjectival root “to be thick” ''*twut.hep ~ twut.kep'' shows the variation ''*h ~ k'', as shown in many other words. The former gave rise to the Middle Korean words {{m|okm|두텁|tr=twùthèp}}, {{m|okm|두텁〯다〮}}, while the latter produced the modern standard form {{ko-l|두껍다}} with loss of the initial consonant in the cluster ''t.k''.

{{w|Alexander Vovin}} alternatively reconstructs original {{IPAfont|*kitpe}} on the strength of the twelfth-century Chinese–Korean wordlist ''{{w|Jilin leishi}}'', which glosses a Korean word transcribed {{m|zh|[[虼]][[鋪]]|tr=*kʰit pʰuə̌}} as {{m|zh|蟇|t=toad}}, and connects this to Tungusic. The {{IPAfont|*twu-}} would then be a prefix.<ref>{{cite-journal|title=Pre-Hankul Materials, Koreo-Japonic, and Altaic|author={{w|Alexander Vovin}}|journal=Korean Studies|year=2000|volume=24|pages=142—155}}</ref> This etymology has issues with explaining the ''twutkep'' form, however, and Korean scholars have generally not accepted it.

