===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|ws|도ᇧ|twòsk}}

For phonological reasons, {{okm-inline|도ᇧ|twòsk}} cannot be a direct ancestor of Modern Seoul Korean {{m|ko|돛}}. Meanwhile, certain dialects preserve a direct reflex of the Middle Korean form, such as Pyongan {{m|ko|돆}}. Rather, the Middle Korean and Modern Seoul Korean forms must descend from a common ancestor, and the coda consonant differences of the two forms reflect the conflation between {{ipachar|/k/}} and {{ipachar|/h/}} which appears to have been current at some stage of the Korean language before Hangul was invented ([[Middle Korean]] {{okm-inline|ㅊ|ch}} behaves as a h-c or c-h consonant cluster). It has been proposed that the Old Korean phonemes *k and *h shared allophones, explaining the conflation.

