{{box|
Beyond Middle Korean, the {{inh|ko|oko|-}} ancestor of this word, tentatively reconstructed as {{IPAfont|<*sèpúl>}}, was the first element of the highest rank of nobility in the ancient Korean kingdom of {{w|Silla}}. This title was variably written with Chinese characters as {{ltc-l|舒發翰|id=1,1,1}} or {{ltc-l|舒弗邯|id=1,1,1}}, representing the sound of the ancient Korean word (note that Korean final {{IPAfont|*-l}}, which does not exist in Chinese, is customarily written with {{IPAfont|-t̚}}) or as {{ltc-l| 角干|id=2,2}} or {{ltc-l|角粲|id=2,1}}, using the Chinese word {{ltc-l|角|id=2|horn}} to represent the meaning of {{IPAfont|<*sèpúl>}}.

However, the same title is also written in forms that imply an initial {{IPAfont|*i-}}, such as {{ltc-l|伊伐湌}} or {{ltc-l|伊罰干}}. This may reflect an alternative word for “horn”, apparently related to as {{IPAfont|<*sèpúl>}}, which was lost by Middle Korean.|Etymological details
