==Korean==

===Etymology===
First attested in the generic "capital" sense in the ''Samguk yusa'' ({{lang|ko|三國遺事 / 삼국유사}}), 1281:

:{{zh-x|collapsed=y|國號 徐羅伐 又 徐伐，今 俗 訓「京」字 云 徐伐，以此 故 也。|tr={{lang|ko|[[국호]][[서라벌]]우[[서벌]]<sup>{{small|[[-이다|-이]][[-니|니]]}}</sup> '''금속훈경자운서벌'''<sup>{{small|[[-은|은]]}}</sup> 이차고야<sup>{{small|[[-라]]}}</sup>}}|The name of the state [later known as {{w|Silla}}] was ''Syelapel'', also ''Syepel''. In contemporary vernacular usage, the character {{m|ko|京|tr=gyeong|t=capital city}} was given the native Korean reading '''''syepel'''''; this usage arose from that earlier name.|3=CL-KR|nocat=y}}

The modern sense of "Seoul" is simply because Seoul was the capital of Korea from 1394 onwards.

{{ko-etym-native|yb|셔〯ᄫᅳᆯ|syěWùl|hangul=y}}

{{ko-etym-native|bdb|셔〯욿|syěwùlh|also=y}}

{{box|
As mentioned in the ''Samguk yusa'', the sense of "capital" is most likely a generalization of the {{inh|ko|oko|-}} place name referring specifically to the capital of {{w|Silla}}, the ancient state which unified Korea. This place name was transcribed using various Chinese characters, most often {{m|ko|徐羅伐|pos=Middle Korean ''Syelapel'', modern ''Seorabeol''}}. This appears to be a compound of:
* the Old Korean proper noun corresponding to ''Silla'', approximated as ''Syela-''. Note that Middle Korean rising pitch, as in ''syě'', is created by a contraction of two syllables, which fits this theory.
* an Old Korean word meaning "town", approximated as ''-pel'', which can be seen in many other ancient toponyms. Note that Middle Korean ''-W-'' comes from former *-p-. Also compare Middle Korean {{okm-l|ᄀᆞ옳|kòWòlh|3=town, district}} and {{okm-l|스〮ᄀᆞᄫᆞᆯ|súkòWòl|3=rural town}}, where this word has survived in fossilized form.{{rfv-etym|okm}}
Hence the word may originally have meant "the town of the Silla".|Etymological details}}

