# {{lb|ko|literary}} [[beloved]] {{q|object of speaker's love}}
#* {{quote-book|ko|year=1926|author={{w|Han Yong-un}}, {{lw|ko|한용운|한용운(韓龍雲)|tr=-}}|title={{lw|ko|님의 침묵|님의 {{ruby|[沈](침)[默](묵)}}|tr=Nim-ui Chimmuk}}|trans-title=The Beloved's Silence|text='''님'''[[-은]] [[가다|갓슴니다]] 아々 [[사랑하다|사랑하는]][[나]][[의]]'''님'''[[-은]] [[가다|갓슴니다]]|tr='''Nim'''eun gatseumnida. Aa, saranghaneun naui '''nim'''eun gatseumnida.|t=My '''beloved''' is gone. Ah, my '''beloved''' that I loved is gone.}}
# {{lb|ko|obsolete}} [[lord]]

====Usage notes====
The prescribed South Korean standard of this word is {{m|ko|임}}, following the South Korean sound rule that Early Modern Korean initial {{IPAchar|/ni/}} is reflected as {{IPAchar|/i/}}. But because this word is often encountered in classical works where this rule was not applied, and because of the cultural impact of works using the ''nim'' variant such as {{w|Han Yong-un}}'s 1926 [[w:ko:님의 침묵|''Nim-ui Chimmuk'']] (''The Beloved's Silence'') and the 1981 pro-democracy song [[w:ko:임을 위한 행진곡|''Nim-eul wihan Haengjingok'']] (''Marching Song for the Beloved''), the ''nim'' variant is actually the more common form.

In North Korea, where the sound rule is not applied, ''nim'' is standard prescriptively as well as descriptively.

