싶다:
==Korean==

===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|wc|식브다〮|sìkpù-tá}} The earliest Middle Korean uses are semantically different, with the meaning "to seem to be about to do".

The adjective was originally derived from {{inh|ko|oko|爲只|tr=*SIk-|t=to [[act]]}} by the adjective-deriving suffix {{okm-l|브|-pu}}, but has since become a single morpheme. Thus related to {{m|ko|시키다|t=to [[cause]] to [[do]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{ko-IPA|a=y}}

===Adjective===
{{ko-adj}}

# {{lb|ko|after the verb suffix {{m|ko|-고}}}} to be [[desirous]] of, to [[want]]
#: {{ux|ko|^마이크로소프트[[사]][[-와]][[-의]] [[거래]][[-에]] [[관하다|관하여]] [[함께]] [[검토하다|검토하고]] '''싶습니다'''.|I''''d like''' to review the Microsoft account with you.}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[공원]][[-에]] [[정말]] [[가다|가고]] '''싶다'''.|[I] really '''want''' to go the park.}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[정말]] [[갖다|갖고]] '''싶은''' [[상품]][[들]]|products that [I] really '''want''' to own}}
# {{n-g|Used after a clause with a sentence ending that expresses a sentiment or subjective speculation, commonly {{m|ko|-ㄴ가}}, {{m|ko|-나}}, {{m|ko|-ㄹ까}}, {{m|ko|-으면}}, and {{m|ko|-다}}, to state that the subject feels that certain way.}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[내일]] [[비]][[-가]] [[오다|왔]][[-으면|으면]] '''싶다'''.|[I] '''feel like''' [I] want rainfall tomorrow.}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[어머니]][[-는]] [[저게]] 뭔가 '''싶어''' 하셨다.|Mother '''was''' confused as to what that was.}}
#: {{ux|ko|[[운동]][[-을]] [[안]] 했으니 [[살]][[-이]] 쪘겠지 '''싶어'''.|[I] '''feel like''' [I] gained weight because [I] didn't exercise.}}

====Usage notes====
Unlike the English word "want", {{m-self|ko|싶다}} can only take a verb. To convey the meaning of "want something" with nouns, use {{m|ko||[[갖다|갖고]] '''싶다'''|lit='''want''' to have}} or {{ko-l|원하다|願—}}.

