삼다:
==Korean==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|ko|okm|삼〯다〮|tr=sǎm-tá}}, from {{inh|ko|oko|沙音|tr=*sam-}}. {{ko-etym-native|ss|삼〯다〮|sǎm-tá|hangul=y}}

When expressing the sense of "to take A as B", Old Korean appears to have natively used the expression "<big>A</big>{{m|oko|乙}} <big>B</big>{{m|oko|沙音}}", with A taking the accusative particle {{m|oko|乙|tr=*-ol}} and B combining directly with the verb *sam- without an intervening particle.

Old Korean texts of any reasonable length are all close translations of a Chinese original, and the [[Literary Chinese]] text often used {{m|zh|以|t=to use}} in the construction "{{m|zh|以}}<big>A</big>{{m|zh|爲}}<big>B</big>" for the sense of "to take A as B". These constructions were translated into Old Korean as "<big>A</big>{{m|oko|乙}} {{m|oko|以}}{{m|oko|厼}} <big>B</big>{{m|oko|沙音}}", with A continuing to take the accusative particle but now becoming the object of the verb {{m|oko|以|tr=*PSU-}} instead, with the literal meaning "to take as B while using A".

In Middle Korean, however, "to take A as B" is generally expressed as "<big>A</big>{{m|okm|-로}} <big>B</big>{{m|okm|-ᄅᆞᆯ}} {{m|okm|삼다}}", with B taking the accusative particle {{okm-l|-ᄅᆞᆯ|-lol}} and A now taking the instrumental particle {{okm-l|-로|-lwo}}. An Ye-ri theorizes that this was the result of syntactic influence from the Chinese "{{m|zh|以}}<big>A</big>{{m|zh|爲}}<big>B</big>" construction, as Chinese {{m|zh|以}} was usually perceived as being equivalent to the Korean instrumental particle {{okm-l|-로|-lwo}}. Thus speakers may have initially imitated the Chinese syntax by using the instrumental particle for A rather than the accusative, after which B was [[reanalyzed]] as being the direct object of {{okm-l|삼〯다〮|sǎm-tá}}.

For unknown reasons, the particles were switched in the early twentieth century, producing the contemporary Korean construction "<big>A</big>{{m|ko|-를|tr=-}} <big>B</big>{{m|ko|-로|tr=-}} {{m|ko|삼다|tr=-}}".

====Pronunciation====
{{ko-IPA|l=y|com=1}}
