{{ko-etym-native|ss|것ᄫᅡᅀᅵ〮|kèsWàzí}} The {{w|Jeju language}} forms {{m|jje|걸버시}} and {{m|jje|걸바시}}, which are clearly cognate to but cannot be directly descended from the Middle Korean form (which show later inter-sonorant lenition of {{IPAchar|/p/}} and {{IPAchar|/s/}} to {{IPAchar|/β/}} and {{IPAchar|/z/}}), implies that the first element was derived from Sino-Korean {{m|ko|乞|tr=geol|t=to beg}}.

The intermediary Middle Korean form {{okm-inline|거ᇫ워ᅀᅵ|kezwezi}} is attested in the later fifteenth century. The first {{IPAchar|/z/}} was regularly deleted sometime in the sixteenth century, while the second underwent an irregular [[fortition]] to {{IPAchar|/ts/}}, producing the Early Modern forms {{ko-inline|거어지}} and {{ko-inline|거워지}}. These coalesced into {{ko-inline|거지}} towards the end of the nineteenth century.

===Pronunciation===
{{ko-IPA|l=y}}
* {{ko-tone|HL}}

===Noun===
{{ko-noun}}

# [[beggar]], [[bum]], [[vagrant]], [[vagabond]], [[hobo]], [[panhandler]]
# {{lb|ko|slang|offensive}} [[blighter]]; [[jerk]]

