뫼:
{{character info}}
{{ko-symbol-nav}}
==Korean==

===Etymology===
{{ko-attest|oko-lat|每|계림유사}}. {{ko-etym-native|1|묗〯|mwǒyh|hangul=y|dot=,}} from pre-MK {{IPAfont|<*mwòrV́h>}}.

The more archaic, perhaps dialectal Middle Korean form {{okm-l|*모로〮|*mwòlwó}} is attested as part of the place name {{okm-l|피〮모로〮|Phímwòlwó}} in the 1447 ''{{w|Yongbi eocheonga}}''. Beyond Korean sources, the earliest attestations are given in eighth-century {{cog|ojp|-}} documents as {{ja-r|[[山#Etymology 3|山]]|むれ}}, presumably reflecting the {{inh|ko|oko|-}} pronunciations of the era.<ref>{{cite-web|author=Rei Fukui|work=ja:小倉進平『朝鮮語方言の研究』所載資料による言語地図とその解釈―第２集|url=http://www.l.u-tokyo.ac.jp/~fkr/小倉進平『朝鮮語方言の研究』所載資料による言語地図とその解釈―第２集.pdf|publisher=ja:東京大学人文社会系研究科 韓国朝鮮文化研究室|page=5-8|date=2018-03-30}}</ref> Compare {{m|ko|모롱이|t=curve of a mountain spur}}.

The original "mountain" sense has been mostly displaced by [[Sino-Korean]] {{m|ko|산(山)}}, which began to drive out the native word in the eighteenth century. The "tomb" sense is from the nineteenth century, reinforced by the unrelated Sino-Korean {{ko-l|묘|墓|tomb}}.

