===Pronunciation===
{{ko-IPA}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ko-etym-native|ss|부텨|Pwùthyè|dot=,}} from {{inh|ko|oko-lat|佛體|tr=*PWUthye?}}, from {{inh|ko|qfa-kor-pro|*Pwutukye}}, which was also loaned into {{cog|mnc|ᡶᡠᠴᡳᡥᡳ||Buddha}} and {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|仏|ほとけ|Buddha}} when Koreans spread Buddhism to those peoples. See the Proto-Korean entry for more.<ref>{{cite-book|first=Alexander|last=Vovin|chapter=Why Manchu and Jurchen Look So Un-Tungusic|title=Tumen jalafun secen aku. Manchu Studies in Honour of Giovanni Stary|editors=Alessandra Pozzi, Juha Janhunen and Michael Weiers|location=Wiesbaden|publisher=Harrassowitz|year=2006|chapterurl=https://www.academia.edu/1804227/Why_Manchu_and_Jurchen_Look_so_Un-Tungusic|pages=255-266}}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7817/jameroriesoci.134.4.689|title=The Awakened Lord: The Name of the Buddha in East Asia|first=Thomas|last=Pellard|journal=Journal of the American Oriental Society|year=2014|doi=10.7817/jameroriesoci.134.4.689}}</ref>  The first two syllables are ultimately from {{der|ko|sa|बुद्ध|tr=buddhá}}, via {{der|ko|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|佛陀}} or some similar Sinitic form. Thomas Pellard speculates that the final element is an ancient Koreanic word for "king; lord".
[[File:Seokguram Buddha.JPG|thumb|{{m-self|ko|^부처}}]]

The traditional etymology still given in Korean dictionaries is that this is a non-Sino-Korean form of the Chinese phrase {{ltc-l|佛體}}, meaning "body of the Buddha", but this is problematic both phonologically (pitch accent and vocalism mismatch) and historically (inability to explain the match between Japanese and Manchu without positing a Korean source for both).

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ko|부텨||Early Modern}}

====Proper noun====
{{ko-proper noun}}

# [[Buddha]] {{gloss|Siddhartha Gautama, founder of Buddhism}}
#: {{syn|ko|^붓다|q1=less common, intentional Sanskritization|^불타(佛陀)|q2=less common}}

====Noun====
{{ko-noun}}

# a [[Buddha]] {{gloss|an enlightened being in Buddhism}}
#: {{syn|ko|^붓다|q1=less common, intentional Sanskritization|^불타(佛陀)|q2=less common}}

