고지식하다:
==Korean==

===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|ws|고디〮식다〮|kwòtí-sìktá|hangul=y}}

The verb was originally a phrase involving the adverb {{okm-l|고디〮|kwòtí}} "in a straight way; righteously" and {{inh|ko|oko|爲只|tr=*SIk-|t=to act}}, thus "to act righteously". Early Hangul texts preserve this positive meaning. However, by the late sixteenth century, the verb had eventually shifted to the adjectival "to be self-righteous; to be stubborn".

Once the word had become an adjective and the Old Korean etymology had long been forgotten, it was misunderstood as a corruption of an adjective formed by the adjective-deriving suffix {{m|ko|하다}}, leading to the modern form via [[hypercorrection]]. This began to occur in the sixteenth century, and the original non-''hada'' form had disappeared by the seventeenth century.

Equivalent to modern {{af|ko|고지식|-하다|t2=to do|pos2=[[light verb]] deriving adjectives}}.

