==Korean==

===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|pts|화냥년|hwanyang-nyen}}

From {{compound|ko|화냥(花娘)|t1=prostitute|년|t2=bitch; derogatory slur for a woman}}. The first element was {{m|okm|화냐ᇰ}} in Middle Korean, but underwent a regular sound shift to modern {{m|ko|화랑(花娘)}}. {{m|ko||화냥년}} was unaffected by this change because of its vulgar nature and retained the Middle Korean pronunciation.

{{w|Folk etymology}} erroneously claims that the first element is {{m|ko|환향(還鄕)|t=to come home}}, and that it was used to refer to women who were taken captive by the {{w|Manchus}} during the {{w|Qing invasion of Joseon|Qing invasion in 1636}} and were eventually sent home, only to be rejected by their families who considered them sexually tainted by the barbaric Manchus.

