오른손:
==Korean==

===Etymology===
{{ko-etym-native|ss|올〮ᄒᆞᆫ손〮|wólhòn-swón|dot=,}} equivalent to {{compound|ko|오른|손|t1=right-side|t2=hand}}, ultimately equivalent to a {{univerbation|ko|[[옳다|옳은]]|[[손]]|t1=which is correct, which is proper|t2=hand|nocap=y}}.

By the early twentieth century, the prestige Central dialect had mostly shifted to the innovative form {{m|ko|바른손|t=right hand}}. However, for unclear reasons, the older but now dialectal form {{m|ko||오른손}} was chosen as the Standard Korean form when the standardized variety was codified in the 1930s, rather than the Central form {{m|ko||바른손}}. In most other cases, Central forms were standardized even when innovative.<ref name="Yi" />

Perhaps because of this standardization and perhaps because of the influx of southern speakers into Central regions during South Korean industrialization, {{m|ko||바른손}} went into decline and is now not generally understood, even after it was belatedly added to the Standard Korean lexicon in the 1980s.<ref name="Yi">{{cite-web|title=ko:'바른손'과 '오른손'|work=korean.go.kr|publisher={{w|National Institute of the Korean Language}}|author=ko:이승재|url=https://www.korean.go.kr/nkview/news/9/3.htm}}</ref>

