* {{m-self|ko|-느니}} is used for all verbs, for the existential adjectives {{m|ko|있다}}, {{m|ko|없다}}, and {{m|ko|계시다}}, and for adjectives with tense marking with {{m|ko|-었-}} and {{m|ko|-겠-}}.
* {{m|ko|-으니}} is used for adjectives without tense marking.

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alter|ko|-으니|-니||See Usage notes}}

===Etymology 3===
{{anchor|verb-final}}
{{ko-etym-native|ws|[[-으니|-으〮니〮/ᄋᆞ〮니]]|-úní/óní|dot=,}} a general-purpose declarative and interrogative suffix of a middling level of deference.

In Middle Korean, this suffix is formally identical to {{okm-l|[[-으니-|-으〮니〮/ᄋᆞ〮니]]|-úní-/óní-}}, a "[[durative]]" [[aspect]]-marking suffix indicating continuous action or timeless fact. Said suffix is etymologically from {{der|ko|oko|隱|tr=*-un|pos=realis gerundive suffix}} + {{m|oko|是|tr=*i-|pos=copula|t=to be}}: "to be that..." ({{ko-ref|Lee and Ramsey 2011, p. 211}}).

The traditional analysis is that this suffix is in fact fully identical to the durative suffix, with the expected formality- and mood-marking suffixes that should follow it being omitted due to the reduced degree of formality. However, it has been argued that this analysis is strange from the perspective of Korean grammar, and that there is in fact a formality-marking suffix {{okm-l|-이〮|-i}} which merges with {{okm-l|-니〮-|-ní-}} due to the shared vowel.<ref>{{cite-journal|title=ko:중세국어 종결어미 '(-으)이'의 분석과 그 문법사적 의의|trans-title=Analysis of the Middle Korean sentence ender ''(-u)i'' and its significance in grammatical history|author=ko:장윤희|journal=Gugeohak|volume=30|year=1997|pages=103—140}}</ref>

Before verbs, the suffix now takes {{m|ko||느}}, from {{cog|okm|-}} {{okm-l|-ᄂᆞ-|-no-}}, originally a Middle Korean present-tense suffix now attached generally to verbs (or tense-marking suffixes derived from verbs) in many grammaticalized suffixes. See Usage notes below.

